               "text": "Keyser, P. (1988)",
               "page": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/263273727_The_Origin_of_the_Latin_Numerals_1_to_1000",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "1988",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "a372974f-4cea-4702-852d-40e70e155583",
               "text": "Wheelock, F. (ed. R. LaFleur)",
               "page": "https://www.csun.edu/~hcfll004/latin-numbers.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2005",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### One by One\n\nLike Mesoamerican numbers, Etruscan-Roman numbers use one symbol for units (I). We can use the same logic to write the first four numbers:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"missing-numbers-table-1\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Table 1: Comparison of Etruscan-Roman, Hindu-Arabic, and Mesoamerican numbers from 1 to 4.*",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "26ca7465-708f-4ec0-b56d-e04ec5a1ca04",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "26ca7465-708f-4ec0-b56d-e04ec5a1ca04",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "inlove",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Different Symbols, the Same Number\n\nFor the number 5, both peoples had a special symbol. The Etruscan-Roman system used half of its symbol for 10 (X). The Mesoamerican system used a bar — which we suppose represents five fingers held together.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"missing-numbers-five\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "2ea7e2aa-4103-4334-8323-0026216f106b",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "2ea7e2aa-4103-4334-8323-0026216f106b",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
