            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "a372974f-4cea-4702-852d-40e70e155583",
               "text": "Wheelock, F. (ed. R. LaFleur)",
               "page": "https://www.csun.edu/~hcfll004/latin-numbers.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2005",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Next Numbers\n\nWith Mesoamerican numbers, we saw that the bar (5) stays and units are added on top to form the next four numbers. Etruscan-Roman numbers use the same logic.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"missing-numbers-table-2\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "*Table 2: Comparison of Etruscan-Roman, Hindu-Arabic, and Mesoamerican numbers from 5 to 9.*",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "f403c4d1-e1f0-40a7-a19f-d0cabf2d7313",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "f403c4d1-e1f0-40a7-a19f-d0cabf2d7313",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Let Us Think!\n\nThe logic behind the first numbers is the same in both systems. Does this mean one of them borrowed the idea from the other?",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4d2971d9-d6d6-4e7f-9f49-d2ee220d6d2e",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "no",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4d2971d9-d6d6-4e7f-9f49-d2ee220d6d2e",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Lack of Evidence\n\nThis question suggests a connection between the peoples — an idea we dismissed in a previous class.\n\nBuilding numbers the same way does not mean the peoples were in contact.\n\nThe formats are also different, and the logic of Etruscan-Roman numbers changes for large numbers.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "4d9543db-8997-4f15-bf3e-4ceafe3ee925",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "4d9543db-8997-4f15-bf3e-4ceafe3ee925",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Number 10\n\nAs we saw, the symbol for 5 (V) comes from the symbol for 10 (X). Mesoamerican numbers, on the other hand, keep the same logic used for 1 to 5.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p data-custom=\"missing-numbers-ten\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "27d5eb4b-f9a9-4816-b211-ab7c94aaecfc",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "27d5eb4b-f9a9-4816-b211-ab7c94aaecfc",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=d603e3a0-cf2a-497d-9fc3-cfed73743515",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we saw that Etruscan-Roman numbers follow a logic similar to Mesoamerican numbers. But there is no evidence that the two peoples borrowed from each other — even though they lived at the same time.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
